<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Harriet Tubman</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1822–<a href="/years/1913">1913</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Araminta Ross</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Harriet Tubman was an American abolitionist and social activist who was born into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> in Dorchester County, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maryland">Maryland</a>. After escaping to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philadelphia">Philadelphia</a> in 1849, she made some thirteen missions back to rescue about seventy enslaved people, among them members of her family, through the network known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/underground-railroad">Underground Railroad</a>. During the Civil War she served the Union Army as an armed scout and spy, and guided the raid at Combahee Ferry in 1863, which freed more than 700 people. In her later years she worked for women's suffrage.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/harriet-tubman.jpg" alt="Harriet Tubman" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Horatio Seymour Squyer · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Harriet_Tubman_(circa_1885).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Harriet Tubman was born Araminta Ross, called Minty, to enslaved parents, Harriet Green, known as Rit, and Ben Ross. The exact year and place of her birth are unknown. She gave 1825, her death certificate lists 1815 and her gravestone 1820, but the biographer Kate Larson, working from a midwife's payment and other documents, recorded 1822, and recent biographies accept March 1822 as most likely.</p><p>As a child she was hired out as a nursemaid and whipped when the baby cried. Early in life an overseer threw a heavy metal weight at another enslaved person and struck her on the head. The injury caused dizziness, pain and spells of sleep for the rest of her life, and she ascribed the visions that followed to God. Around 1844 she married John Tubman, a free Black man.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1849 Tubman fell ill, which lowered her value to slave traders, and Edward Brodess tried to sell her. After his death his widow began selling the people the family enslaved, and Tubman escaped to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philadelphia">Philadelphia</a>. She then returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maryland">Maryland</a> to bring out her family, one group at a time, and guided dozens of others, travelling by night and usually in winter. Called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moses">Moses</a>, she later said she never lost a passenger. After the Fugitive Slave Act of 1850 she guided escapees on to Southern Ontario. In April 1858 she met John Brown and helped him plan his raid on Harpers Ferry in 1859.</p><p>In January 1862 she volunteered to help refugees at Port Royal, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-carolina">South Carolina</a>. In early 1863 she led scouts there under orders of Secretary of War Edwin Stanton, and later that year she guided three steamboats with Black soldiers under Colonel James Montgomery up the Combahee River. Enslaved people warned by her network understood from the steamboat whistles that they were being liberated. She received a total of $200 for over three years of service. In her later years she spoke for women's suffrage and worked with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/susan-b-anthony">Susan B. Anthony</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Tubman died on 10 March 1913, after being admitted to a home for elderly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-americans">African Americans</a> that she had helped establish. Her unofficial status as a scout, spy and nurse made her service hard to document, and she lived in poverty, since she kept using her money for her family and for formerly enslaved people. She is commonly viewed as an icon of courage and freedom.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Harriet Tubman” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…who escaped from slavery in Maryland in 1838, became one of the movement’s most famous orators and founded his own newspaper; <b>Harriet Tubman</b>, who escaped in 1849, went back some thirteen times to lead about seventy people to freedom; Sojourner Truth preached against slavery across the northern states.</blockquote><a href="/movement/abolitionism">Abolitionism · People</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Harriet Tubman</b>, who had escaped slavery, was one of the most famous and successful conductors.</blockquote><a href="/movement/underground-railroad">Underground Railroad · People</a></li><li><blockquote>The authorised biography of <b>Harriet Tubman</b> quotes her as saying she used it as a code song for people escaping slavery.</blockquote><a href="/essays/how-songs-travel">How Songs Travel</a></li><li><blockquote>Sarah Bradford's authorised biography of <b>Harriet Tubman</b>, Scenes in the Life of Harriet Tubman (1869), quotes Tubman as saying she used &quot;Go Down Moses&quot; as one of two code songs to communicate with people escaping slavery from Maryland.</blockquote><a href="/songs/go-down-moses">Go Down Moses</a></li><li><blockquote>It is believed that <b>Harriet Tubman</b>, who made thirteen trips to the South and helped free more than 70 people, used it to warn people escaping to leave the trail and go into the water, so that the slave catchers' dogs could not follow their scent; a 2002…</blockquote><a href="/songs/wade-in-the-water">Wade in the Water</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Dorchester County, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. Died in Auburn, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=38.42,-76.08&amp;year=1881" aria-label="Dorchester County on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2659 784 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Harriet Tubman"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3109 1054h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3109 1054h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=38.42,-76.08&amp;year=1881">What was here in 1881? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;I looked at my hands to see if I was de same pusson. There was such a glory ober ebery ting; de sun came like gold through the trees, and ober the fields, and I felt like I was in Heaben.&#8221;</p><footer>On first crossing into freedom, 1849 (Scenes in the Life of Harriet Tubman, 1869) &#183; original &#183; as Sarah H. Bradford recorded her telling, in dialect &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/57821/pg57821-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;But I was free, and they should be free. I would make a home in the North and bring them there, God helping me.&#8221;</p><footer>On first crossing into freedom, 1849 (Scenes in the Life of Harriet Tubman, 1869) &#183; original &#183; as Sarah H. Bradford recorded her telling &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/57821/pg57821-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;I had reasoned dis out in my mind; there was one of two things I had a right to, liberty, or death; if I could not have one, I would have de oder; for no man should take me alive…&#8221;</p><footer>On her escape from slavery (Harriet, the Moses of Her People, 1886) &#183; original &#183; as Sarah H. Bradford recorded her telling, in dialect &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/9999/pg9999-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>1 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;I was de conductor ob de Underground Railroad for eight years, an' I can say what mos' conductors can't say—I nebber run my train off de track an' I nebber los' a passenger.&#8221;</p><footer>To a women's suffrage meeting in Rochester (Harriet, the Moses of Her People, 1886) &#183; original &#183; as Sarah Bradford recorded it &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/9999/pg9999-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
